---
import OptionPage from '../../components/OptionPage.astro';
import prices from '../../data/option-prices.json';
import { link } from '../../data/site.js';

const page = {
  path: '/options/fascia-trim-accents/',
  name: 'Fascia, Trim & Decorative Accents',
  title: 'Shed Fascia, Trim, Shutters, Brackets & Exterior Accents | Appalachian Sheds',
  description: 'Fascia, rake, corner and casing trim for Appalachian Sheds buildings in LP SmartSide, cellular PVC or cedar, plus shutters, flower boxes, timber brackets and gable medallions, with planning prices.',
  h1: 'Fascia, Trim & Decorative Accents',
  answer: 'Trim finishes the roof edges, corners, doors and windows and sets the color breaks that make a building look deliberate. LP SmartSide trim is the standard material, at $9 to $18 per linear foot; cellular PVC runs $16 to $30 and western red cedar $18 to $34. Decorative accents such as shutters ($220–$650 a pair), cedar flower boxes and timber brackets are layered on once the siding, trim, roofline and colors are settled.',
  familyLabel: 'Exterior Detail',
  stepsTitle: 'The Trim Goes On Late, but the Trim Plan Starts Early',
  stepsLead: 'Trim affects siding layout, roof-edge depth, window and door balance, gable details, paint lines and caulking. Changing it late ripples through the rest of the exterior.',
  steps: [
    ['Confirm the style', 'Storage, workshop, cottage, office, studio or poolside.'],
    ['Choose the material', 'LP, PVC, cedar, fiber cement, pine or a planned mix.'],
    ['Set the widths', 'Fascia, rake, corners, casing and gable details.'],
    ['Plan color breaks', 'Body, trim, door and accent colors.'],
    ['Review water details', 'Caulking, flashing, drainage paths and cut-end protection.'],
    ['Add accents last', 'Shutters, boxes, brackets and gable details, with blocking planned.'],
  ],
  families: [
    {
      id: 'materials',
      name: 'Trim Materials',
      intro: [
        'LP SmartSide trim suits most painted buildings. Cellular PVC resists moisture and suits offices, studios, pool houses and fascia-heavy elevations, but needs correct expansion and fastening details. Western red cedar gives natural Appalachian character with a real stain, seal, paint or weathering plan.',
        'Proportion matters as much as material: trim that is too narrow looks unfinished, and trim that is too wide overpowers a small building.',
      ],
      prices: prices.trim.materials,
    },
    {
      id: 'packages',
      name: 'Trim Packages',
      intro: [
        'Trim is priced by the linear foot or as a defined package once the building size, roofline, openings, material and finish are known. Taller walls, steep pitches, multiple gables and added openings all increase the scope.',
      ],
      prices: prices.trim.packages,
      after: 'Planning ranges, not quotes. Trim planned with the original build is cleaner and less costly than modifying finished siding later.',
    },
    {
      id: 'accents',
      name: 'Decorative Exterior Accents',
      intro: [
        'Accents are the character layer: shutters sized to the window, flower boxes under the right window, brackets where the eye expects support, and gable details centered and sized to the gable face. A few correct accents look more premium than many unrelated ones.',
        'Flower boxes, brackets and medallions are stronger and cleaner when wall blocking is planned before construction. Simple painted flower boxes are listed with the Windows options.',
      ],
      prices: prices.trim.accents,
    },
  ],
  compare: {
    title: 'Every Trim Position Has a Job',
    head: ['Trim', 'Primary job', 'What it controls visually'],
    rows: [
      ['Fascia', 'Finishes the roof edge and covers framing', 'Roofline depth and visual weight'],
      ['Rake boards', 'Finish the sloped gable edges', 'Gable outline'],
      ['Frieze boards', 'Transition at the top of the wall', 'Horizontal definition below the roof'],
      ['Corner boards', 'Protect siding edges at corners', 'Vertical strength and proportion'],
      ['Window & door casing', 'Frame the openings', 'Window presence and entry emphasis'],
      ['Band board', 'Breaks taller wall planes', 'Material and color transitions'],
      ['Gable trim', 'Defines the gable area', 'Vents, brackets, medallions'],
    ],
  },
  considerationsTitle: 'Trim & Accent Mistakes to Avoid',
  considerations: [
    ['Choosing trim too late', 'Trim should guide siding layout and color breaks.'],
    ['Blind caulking', 'Seal approved joints; keep lap and drainage paths open.'],
    ['Too many colors or accents', 'A disciplined body-and-trim palette looks more premium.'],
    ['Undersized shutters or cupolas', 'Accents sized too small look like afterthoughts.'],
    ['Flower boxes on siding alone', 'Wet soil is heavy; boxes need proper backing.'],
    ['Paint, Stain & Finish Systems', 'Trim color, coating path and upkeep.', link('/options/paint-stain-finish/')],
  ],
  faqs: [
    { q: 'What is the difference between fascia, rake and frieze trim?', a: 'Fascia runs along a roof edge, rake trim follows the sloped edge of a gable, and frieze trim is a horizontal transition near the top of the wall.' },
    { q: 'Is PVC trim worth the upgrade?', a: 'It can be on offices, pool houses, porch details and fascia-heavy designs where moisture resistance and lower maintenance matter. It runs $16 to $30 per linear foot.' },
    { q: 'Can trim or accents be added later?', a: 'Sometimes, but it is cleaner and less costly to plan them before siding, paint, windows and blocking are finalized.' },
    { q: 'Should trim be a different color than the siding?', a: 'Often a disciplined body color and trim color works best. Too many competing colors make a small building feel busy.' },
  ],
  ctaTitle: 'Plan the Exterior Details With Edwin',
};
---
<OptionPage page={page} />
